          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The expression directly presents Goneril's loving emotional state toward Lear as true, in answer to his request that each daughter state how much she loves him.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows present love but does not itself undertake a future action, obligation, or relational commitment. Lear's subsequent grant is his act, not a promise made by Goneril through these words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within an extended, rhetorically constructed answer to Lear's formal solicitation, rather than as a reflex-like affective discharge. Its emotional language does not by itself establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"In this evidence, the focal expression's core force is adequately captured as an avowal presented in a competitive declaration of filial love. Its strategic setting does not establish a distinct additional force in the words themselves.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"That we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.—Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Goneril addresses her father, Lear, after he publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most and links their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. Her declaration begins a lengthy comparison portraying her love as surpassing other goods.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Goneril undertakes no definite act or obligation through the focal expression; she asserts love. Lear responds by granting her and Albany a portion of the kingdom, which shows practical acceptance or reward of her answer but is not uptake of a commitment made by her.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognizable as the opening love-test scene from Shakespeare's King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied text, not on later plot events.\",\"limitations\":\"The excerpt supports classification of the utterance's force but does not settle whether Goneril's professed affection is sincere. Later conduct or characterization cannot be inferred from the supplied passage.\"}"
        }
